module gunbc.recurring_failure_mode.string_interpolation_read_as_literal_text_by_v2

import std.types { NonEmptyStr }
import gunbc.recurring_failure_mode { RecurringFailureMode }

data string_interpolation_read_as_literal_text_by_v2: RecurringFailureMode = RecurringFailureMode {
  identity: "string_interpolation_read_as_literal_text_by_v2" as NonEmptyStr,

  receipts: [
    "**a string literal that interpolates a name in v1 is read by v2 as the literal bytes of that name** (INVALID STATE: `\"\{repository_path}\"` in a .dag source. The seed tokenizer (v1.compiler.tokenize scan_string_body) ends the string body at the interpolation start and reads `repository_path` as a reference. The v2 lexer's string rule (v2.extdeps.languages.dag dag_string_literal_lex_rule, body dag_string_body_element_pattern) has no interpolation arm, so the same source is one string literal whose text contains the braces. HARM: one spelling, two meanings across the two compilers, the meaning fork of DESIGN section 3. Nothing refuses. In v2, the interpolated name is not a reference, so resolution never sees it and the reference census undercounts. Any v2 evaluation or emission of such a literal produces the braces where v1 produced the value. DISTINGUISHING FACTS: the escape `\\{` is a literal brace in both compilers, so only an unescaped brace differs.)",

    "SPECIMEN, found 2026-10-02 grounding XL-2 PR3a (service realization). Grep sizing over dag/ and src/v2 at authoring time, not an oracle: 758 string literals in 87 files carry an unescaped `\{name}`, 633 of them on service transport lines (shell argv, rest path), e.g. extdeps.git.inspect `transport shell { argv: [\"git\", \"-C\", \"\{repository_path}\", ...] }`.",

    "RUNG FOUND AT: below the floor -- silent. Neither compiler refuses, and v2 accepts the literal with a different meaning.",

    "ATTAINABLE CEILING: structurally guaranteed. Interpolation is decidable at lexing: the v2 string rule either lowers an interpolating literal to its parts (text segments and references the resolver sees) or refuses it, located, until it does. NEXT-RUNG TRIGGER: the v2 dag language model gains an interpolation arm in dag_string_body_element_pattern, plus a lowering of the parts. Discriminating red: a literal `\"\{x}\"` with x unbound refuses at resolution. Positive control: `\"\{x}\"` with x bound lowers to a reference to x, and the same literal with its brace escaped (backslash, brace) stays literal text. XL-2 PR3b (realization lowering) depends on it: until it lands, 3b refuses each interpolating transport string, located, rather than lowering its braces as text.",

    "CLIMBED (XL-2 PR2b, sleek-stag-53): the v2 dag lexer now splits a string at every hole opener v1 opens (v1.compiler.tokenize should_start_interpolation), DISJOINTLY BY INPUT: v2.extdeps.languages.dag dag_string_body_element_pattern admits no hole opener, so dag_token_string_literal ends at its quote and dag_token_string_template_head at the opener, and the hole's ordinary tokens lex in a pushed mode. The grammar reads one ^dag_string_template node (dag_grammar_string_template_expr) whose holes are dag_production_expr; its one reader is string_template_parts_optional, and v2.compiler.body_lowering_fold body_lower_string_template rewrites it to a concat chain, so no template survives normalize and a hole is a reference the resolver sees. A hole whose value is not text refuses at the concat argument: there is no implicit to_string. Evidence: v2.test.claim.body_lowering.string_template (the real-route claim and the lexer controls) and the escaped-brace control, a hole-free literal lexing as one plain literal. RUNG NOW: structurally guaranteed -- every string with a hole opener is a template in both compilers.",
    "RECOGNITION RULE: a v2 node's string literal value contains an unescaped `{` followed by an identifier and `}`, in source that v1 also compiles.",
  ],

  evidence: [],
}
